Charge-magnitude scaling: does the even part go as q^2?
====================================================================================================
site                     q   1st order        /q   2nd order      /q^2
------------------------------------------------------------------------

  The first-order column divided by q, and the second-order column
  divided by q^2, should each be constant down a site block. Constancy
  in the second column confirms the expansion is perturbative at 1 e and
  that every result here transfers to smaller charges by scaling.
  Departure indicates higher-order or charge-transfer contamination,
  which Sokalski (1985) notes has exponential rather than polynomial
  character and would invalidate the parity separation at this magnitude.

====================================================================================================
first-order error (explicit minus q*Dv): mean -4.8223  sd 5.1897  max |e| 10.8360 kcal/mol
second-order (polarisation) term:        mean -4.8845  sd 4.8795  max |t| 10.5487 kcal/mol

  The first-order term is not reproduced within a constant. The error
  scatters, so the ranking is affected and the screen cannot be
  trusted without correction.

  The polarisation term is not systematic across sites. Its scatter
  sets the accuracy the linear objective can claim, and top-ranked
  candidates must be verified explicitly rather than trusted.

